# Vercel Web Analytics Implementation

Successfully implemented Vercel Web Analytics for the Sillah_V2 frontend project.

## Changes Made

### 1. Package Installation
- Added `@vercel/analytics` package (v1.6.1) to the project dependencies
- Used npm to install the package, which updated both `package.json` and `package-lock.json`

### 2. Code Changes
**Modified: `src/App.jsx`**
- Imported the `Analytics` component from `@vercel/analytics/react`
- Added the `<Analytics />` component to the main App component, placed alongside the existing `<SpeedInsights />` component

## Implementation Details

The implementation follows the React (Create React App) pattern from the Vercel documentation:
- Used the `@vercel/analytics/react` package for React applications
- The `Analytics` component is a wrapper around the tracking script that provides seamless integration with React
- Placed the component at the root level of the application to track all route changes
- The component works with the existing React Router setup for automatic route tracking

## Verification

✅ Build completed successfully (`npm run build`)
✅ No new linting errors introduced in `App.jsx`
✅ Package dependencies properly updated
✅ The Analytics component is positioned correctly in the component tree

## Next Steps

After deployment to Vercel:
1. Enable Web Analytics in the Vercel dashboard (Analytics tab → Enable)
2. After the next deployment, verify the `/_vercel/insights/view` endpoint is accessible
3. Monitor analytics data in the Vercel dashboard

## Notes

- The project already had `@vercel/speed-insights` installed, so this addition complements the existing Vercel integrations
- Both Speed Insights and Web Analytics components are now present in the App component
- The implementation preserves all existing code structure and functionality
- Route tracking is automatic thanks to React Router integration
